Throne God: A Throne God is an existence whose individual Origin has attained Taikyoku, becoming a Supreme Ultimate Origin capable of emanating outward and establishing its own governing reality throughout creation. Within the TYPE MOON cosmology, the designation expresses both a condition of existence and a form of divine sovereignty: the individual becomes a living universe or multiverse whose fundamental principle can determine the conditions under which other existences operate. An ordinary person is directed by their Origin, the fundamental orientation underlying their existence. A Throne God reverses the apparent scale of that relationship by establishing a creation directed according to their own Origin, with their deepest Craving determining the arrangement through which that supreme principle becomes a Hegemony Law. The Origin supplies the principle, the Craving supplies its personal orchestration, Taikyoku establishes its supremacy, and the Hegemony Law expresses the resulting order. Consequently, the God’s internal reality acquires cosmological authority, and a desire belonging to one individual becomes an objective condition of existence for innumerable others. The foundation of this sovereignty is 「The Throne」, the incomprehensible intrinsic nucleus of 「　」, conventionally called Akasha or the Root. Descriptions such as nucleus, mechanism, vacancy, and seat are approximations through which differentiated minds attempt to represent something whose nature cannot be exhausted by the language, mathematics, dimensional theories, or causal frameworks belonging to creation. The Throne is inseparable from the Root and possesses no independent standing above it. Its intrinsic functions maintain the distinctions through which phenomena emerging from 「　」 can remain meaningfully differentiated: a particular soul can remain that soul, an Origin can remain distinguishable from its bearer, and one universe can possess a causal history different from another. Through this relationship, plurality remains possible without becoming severed from its ultimate source. The Throne God occupies the relationship of rulership established through this nucleus, allowing their own Origin and Craving to become the governing order of the painted creation. Occupancy does not make the God the owner of Akasha, nor does it establish that every function intrinsic to the Root has become a personal ability of its ruler. Their sovereignty proceeds through the Throne while their individuality remains distinct from 「　」. This distinction becomes extraordinarily small when an Origin attains Taikyoku. The Supreme Ultimate Origin approaches the Absolute Origin so closely that the remaining separation is described as infinitesimal, with the Throne stabilizing that separation and permitting the God to remain an independent consciousness. Such proximity is an approximation of metaphysical relationship, beyond the usefulness of distance or coordinates. Closing the remaining distinction would mean complete return to 「　」, with the disappearance of the separate self whose desire, experience, and orientation constitute the particular God. Their condition is therefore one of supreme individual existence maintained at the final restriction before undifferentiated oneness. Taikyoku expresses this standing through the outward supremacy of the God’s Origin. Its principle precedes and supersedes the ordinary laws and concepts operating within the reality it establishes, freeing its possessor from the frameworks that once defined the limits of their existence. Physical and conceptual dimensions, information, temporal sequence, and distinctions such as life and death or individuality and totality cannot comprehensively define a Throne God. Their transcendence cannot be reduced to the addition of another spatial axis or a greater quantity of magical energy. They establish a different relationship with the conditions under which those measurements become meaningful. A world may be an expression of the God without containing the totality of the God, and the God may remain independent of that world while being present throughout it. Describing them as a walking universe conveys an aspect of this relationship, although the term does not imply that their complete existence is a large collection of celestial objects confined within a humanoid body. Their inner reality possesses its own supreme principle, and emanation extends that principle outward until the painted creation becomes an expression of their will. The relative density of Taikyoku is represented through numerical values. Even a value of one signifies an extraordinary transcendence beyond ordinary material existence, while successive values express greater supremacy by substantial degrees for which no universal arithmetic conversion is established. No fixed maximum value exists. The weight of desire, the strength of the soul and Origin, and the individual mechanisms contributing to their density determine the standing of a particular God, while compatibility influences the confrontation between their Laws. A sufficiently great disparity guarantees the stronger God’s superiority. This hierarchy means that the absoluteness of a Throne God remains intelligible in relation to other possessors of Taikyoku, even where ordinary phenomena cannot challenge them on equal terms. Their Hegemony Law is the expression of that supremacy throughout creation. When established through the Throne, the God’s personal truth becomes the ultimate order governing the painted universes, timelines, parallel histories, higher planes, and subordinate realms within their dominion. Previous physics, causal relationships, temporal conditions, and concepts of life and death can be superseded by the nature of the new world. A Law consequently possesses authority over the circumstances through which events occur, extending beyond the production of an exceptional effect within an otherwise independently defined universe. If an Origin of Water attained Taikyoku and established its principle across creation, the resulting water would not become subject to evaporation or freezing through subordinate natural laws, because the authority determining those processes would already have been superseded. The precise arrangement nevertheless depends upon Craving. Mercurius’s Origin of Unknown, directed through his refusal to accept any ending other than the conclusion he desires, becomes Acta Est Fabula and establishes Heaven Lost, the creation governed by recurrence. Renichi’s Origin of Stagnation and desire to remain within a cherished moment become a Law capable of preventing change throughout existence. Reinhardt’s Origin of Love expresses itself through the eternal warfare, resurrection, and sovereign unity of his Legion. Their shared divine standing therefore coexists with profoundly different worlds, each carrying the desires and contradictions of its ruler. A Throne God’s personality remains cosmologically consequential because the Law is a supreme principle belonging to someone. The nature of the desired world cannot be separated from the individual who desires it. Wherever their Hegemony Law has been painted, the ruling God possesses omnipresence and omniscience within that territory. Their perception extends throughout the creation expressing their will, and the apparent location of a human form does not delimit their presence. A realm locally possessed by another being remains subject to this relationship if it is contained within the painted creation. This territorial completeness does not independently establish exhaustive knowledge of 「　」 or unrestricted access to the Akashic Records. Mercurius’s paradoxical existence, for example, prevents his use of the Memory of the Throne and Akashic Records despite the extraordinary reach of his own Law and accumulated foreknowledge. The individual mechanics of divine existence remain significant even at this level. The unrestricted presence of a fully established Throne God is itself destructive beyond the World’s capacity to endure. Their metaphysical weight cannot be supported by ordinary space, time, or matter, and even a drop of their divine blood is described as weighing more than a neutron star. Such descriptions illustrate the disproportion between the God and the creation receiving them, without making ordinary measurements adequate accounts of their nature. The Throne contains and stabilizes this pressure, supporting the God’s emanation while preserving the creation that would otherwise be torn apart by their presence. The seat consequently serves both its ruler and the world governed by that ruler. A recognizable human appearance provides no assurance that the God’s continued existence depends upon an ordinary heart, brain, or arrangement of organs. Their body, soul, Origin, inner universe, and emanated reality possess relationships that cannot be reduced to conventional anatomy. Particular manifestations must therefore be evaluated according to their actual connection with the divine source. Mercurius’s Sensories are diminished interfaces described as complete nothingness, allowing him to interact with creation while his true self remains seated. Their destruction leaves his underlying existence unharmed. Kaminagara possesses a different relationship: it is the manifest embodiment of the God’s Hegemony Law and inner universe, externalizing their reality through a cosmic form corresponding to their nature. Its apparent scale can be immense, with Mercurius appearing through an ouroboric celestial dragon, Reinhardt through a colossal golden lion associated with his Legion, and Renichi through a vast celestial mechanism of clocks and interlocking machinery. Within the Kaminagara, the God’s Law possesses no blind spots. Damage to it can affect its possessor, although the feedback is limited and does not make the manifestation an ordinary exposed vital organ. Meaningful opposition must reach the standing of the Law being expressed. The same requirement explains why a Throne God’s relationship with death exceeds familiar immortality. Conventional mortality belongs to the differentiated creation superseded by Taikyoku, and destroying a visible body, severing a soul, erasing information, or invoking a concept does not automatically terminate the underlying God. An attack must establish that it can meaningfully apply to their divine existence. Their true defeat ordinarily occurs through another God’s comparable or greater Taikyoku, with the weaker Law being overwritten, crushed, and consumed together with the existence expressing it. The resulting termination can be called death without requiring that ordinary mortality governed the confrontation. Explicit exceptions also exist. 「Shiki Ryougi」 can harm Throne Gods through her particular relationship with 「　」 without possessing Taikyoku herself, and this exception belongs to her specific nature rather than automatically extending to every Root-connected person or wielder of death perception. A True Death Cell provides another route through a mechanism originating within the God’s own soul. Born from a subconscious desire for an ending, it can develop sufficient standing to confront its originator and become a personal embodiment of termination where the conventional Concept of Death has no applicability. Reinhardt’s relationship with Mercurius exemplifies both the power and dependency of this condition, permitting extraordinary confrontation with his source while binding his final fate to that source’s true death. Divine victory must also be distinguished from a permanent conclusion. Mercurius’s recurrence can operate after his defeat and death when the resulting ending remains unacceptable to him, so overcoming his manifested power does not independently guarantee escape from Heaven Lost. Conversely, the true death of a seated ruler does not automatically erase the world established through their Law. The Throne preserves the existing order until a successor successfully establishes another Hegemony Law. Possession of Taikyoku, capacity for emanation, and full enthronement must likewise be distinguished. A candidate or incomplete God may possess a supreme Origin and express a Law while remaining subject to restrictions associated with their particular condition. Renichi and Reinhardt therefore cannot be assigned every attribute of unrestricted seated rulership merely through their relationship with Mercurius. Reaching the Throne introduces further ordeals. Its nature cannot be approached through movement that presupposes a destination within the World, and ordinary teleportation or dimensional travel does not independently establish access to its nucleus. A Singular Finite Curve opens a route by tearing through the framework of reality, with its apparent depth corresponding to the metaphysical difficulty imposed by the reigning God’s standing. Establishing a path to the Root does not guarantee arrival at the Throne, and opening a sufficient rupture does not guarantee survival of its traversal. The challenger must also overcome the Judgement of Mortality, the autonomous annihilating mechanism encountered in the approach to divine rulership, before confronting the seat’s owner. Mercurius became the first mage to discover and claim this condition, barely surviving the approach before his enthronement established the creation governed by his recurrence. The attainment of such supremacy nevertheless preserves the particularity that makes its possessor a God with a personal Law. Knowledge does not inherently supply fulfillment, recurrence does not inherently supply an acceptable ending, and the capacity to preserve a moment does not resolve every contradiction involved in loving those who wish to move beyond it. These remain matters of the individual whose Origin has become cosmological. A Throne God governs through the most extensive expression of that individual truth while the Throne preserves the final distinction through which the ruler can continue to say “I” before 「　」.

CHARACTERISTICS
Physiology: A Throne God is an existence whose individual Origin has attained the condition of Taikyoku, becoming a supreme principle capable of emanating outward and establishing its own reality throughout creation. Its physiology is inseparable from the relationship between the individual, their Origin, and the universe expressed through their Hegemony Law. Although a Throne God may originate as a human and retain a recognizable human appearance, their divine existence cannot be comprehensively described through the biological characteristics of that former condition. Their body, soul, consciousness, and manifested world are expressions of an existence that exceeds the physical and conceptual frameworks ordinarily used to distinguish those things. They are described as walking universes or multiverses because their internal reality possesses cosmological significance of its own, while their emanation renders the creation painted by their Law an extension of their will. However, even this description remains an approximation: the God is independent of the world they establish, yet that world is an expression of their existence, and neither statement exhaustively defines their nature. A Throne God does not require the ordinary conditions of space and time to establish the validity of their existence, and distinctions such as material and spiritual, existence and nonexistence, individuality and totality, or finite and infinite cannot comprehensively contain them. Their apparent location therefore does not describe the full extent of their presence, just as their apparent dimensions do not measure the scope of their being. This independence extends to conventional mortality. A Throne God possesses no ordinary biological lifespan whose expiration would naturally terminate their divine existence, and destroying something recognizable as a body does not necessarily reach the underlying God. The Concept of Death belongs to the differentiated creation governed by their Law; its ordinary applicability does not extend upward merely because the God can be observed in a human form. Their immortality is consequently more fundamental than continuous regeneration or the absence of a familiar vital organ. The same distinction applies to physical, spiritual, informational, and conceptual attacks: each must establish that it can meaningfully reach the God rather than merely affect a manifestation or something within the painted creation. The unrestricted presence of a fully established Throne God is itself catastrophic to the World. Their metaphysical weight exceeds what space, time, and matter can sustain, with even a drop of their divine blood described as weighing more than a neutron star. Such descriptions indicate the disproportion between their existence and the creation receiving it; ordinary measurements of mass or energy cannot comprehensively account for them. 「The Throne」 supports and stabilizes the ruling God, containing the otherwise destructive pressure of their presence while allowing their Law to emanate in an established order. This function simultaneously preserves the God’s individuality and protects the creation governed by them. The condition of a fully seated ruler must nevertheless be distinguished from incomplete godhood or candidacy. Possession of Taikyoku, the capacity to emanate a Hegemony Law, and unrestricted possession of every attribute associated with enthronement are related achievements whose expression varies according to the individual. Renichi and Reinhardt, for example, do not automatically possess every physiological attribute of Mercurius’s fully established rulership merely because they can participate in the same divine order.

Taikyoku: Taikyoku, the Supreme Ultimate Origin, is the condition in which an individual’s Origin becomes supreme relative to the laws and concepts of creation and emanates outward to establish them according to itself. An ordinary Origin directs the fundamental orientation of its bearer; even when awakened, it ordinarily governs the individual whose existence proceeds from that orientation. Taikyoku expands this relationship until creation itself becomes directed according to the individual’s Origin. The Origin supplies the principle, while the individual’s deepest Craving determines the orchestration through which that principle becomes a Hegemony Law. Their respective functions remain inseparable without becoming interchangeable. Mercurius’s Origin of Unknown and his refusal to accept any conclusion other than his desired ending produce the recurrence of Acta Est Fabula; Renichi’s Origin of Stagnation and desire to remain within a cherished moment produce the preservation expressed through his own Law. Taikyoku is therefore both the foundation of divine power and an expression of the particular person possessing it. Its supremacy means that an ordinary phenomenon cannot overturn its principle simply by following a subordinate natural law. If an Origin of Water attained Taikyoku and established its reality across creation, that water would not be compelled to freeze or evaporate by the former operation of temperature, because those processes would no longer possess authority over the principle defining the world. The same relationship extends beyond physics to information, causality, dimensionality, and conceptual distinctions. A God possessing Taikyoku is independent of the frameworks their emanation supersedes; adding further spatial dimensions or increasing the complexity of an informational structure does not independently overcome that relationship. Their distance from ordinary phenomena is sometimes compared to the relationship between a person and an image of fire drawn upon paper. This comparison expresses a difference in existential standing, while remaining an analogy rather than a literal claim that creation is a fictional publication. Differences between Taikyoku are represented through numerical values. Even a value of one signifies transcendence beyond ordinary material existence, and successive values indicate greater supremacy by a substantial degree for which no universal arithmetic conversion is established. The values have no fixed maximum. Their density is associated with the weight of desire and the strength of the soul and Origin, with accumulated souls contributing where the individual’s divine system incorporates them. Compatibility also matters in a confrontation between Laws, although a sufficiently great disparity in Taikyoku guarantees the superiority of the stronger God. Incompatible emanations cannot peacefully define the same creation according to mutually exclusive ultimate principles. Their collision becomes a confrontation of Origins, Cravings, and the realities expressing them, and a sufficiently intense clash can tear open a Singular Finite Curve. Its apparent depth expresses metaphysical difficulty and weight of existence rather than measurable distance. At the furthest extent of the God’s relationship with their own Origin lies the remaining distinction between that Supreme Ultimate Origin and 「　」, the Absolute Origin. This distinction is described as infinitesimally small, with 「The Throne」 maintaining its stability so that the God can remain an individual consciousness. Taikyoku consequently establishes extraordinary proximity to Akasha without making its possessor Akasha itself. A Throne God being the final restriction before 「　」 being the Root. The Root is already impossible to speak of, impossible to even know, because it’s just 「　」, and when a Throne God removes that last infinitesimal proximity between themselves and the Root, they lose all grounds of themselves, and they become one with the Root. The Throne God loses the power of distinctions, self, being, and absolute everything up until there is only 「　」.  Their Supreme Ultimate Origin allows them to preserve an individual identity at a proximity to Akasha where every lesser basis of existence has ceased to provide an adequate description. That means their last restriction isn't a lack of energy, power, dimensional magnitude, or metaphysical authority. It is the fact that they are still someone. And when that distinction disappears, the result is not a stronger Throne God. There is no longer a Throne God to become stronger. There is only:「　」. Fully enthroned Throne Gods is the closest thing possible to this state and they exists as the very final restriction upholding the entire of existence as a world self-sustaining pillar acting as the ground of being everything without being the Root. The difference between a Throne God’s and Void Shiki’s relationship with the Root is that the other is connected to it while the other has infinitesimal proximity to it. Void Shiki relationship to 「　」 is direct connection through the Origin of Emptiness, and she even suggests that she herself may be the Spiral of Origin. Throne God’s relationship to 「　」 is infinitesimal ontological proximity, sustained through the Throne while retaining a distinct Supreme Ultimate Origin without becoming indistinguishable from the Root. They approach the absolute limit of individuality without surrendering that individuality. At their core, a Throne God isn’t bound to all of creation in the Nasuverse. Not the entire observable universe. Not every parallel world. Not an infinitely dimensional cosmos. Not the Throne of Heroes, Imaginary Number Space, or the whole collection of physical and conceptual laws because they exist completely independently from it, as while as being as all of creation in the Nasuverse, while at the same they are not, and at the same time they above it while they don’t even exist as above as everything above, independent, and being everything are all approximation because They are immanent throughout their creation without being confined to it, independent of its conditions without being adequately described as exterior to it, and transcendent without transcendence constituting the entirety of their nature because if a Throne God is very final restriction besides the Root, then what could possible exhaust their if everything else in the Nasuverse’s Space, Time, World and Key Terminology couldn’t besides the Root.


Abilities: The foundational ability of a Throne God is Emanation: the outward establishment of their internal reality as the governing order of creation. Through 「The Throne」, the ruling God’s Origin and Craving become a Hegemony Law that determines the nature, structure, and operation of painted world from the moment of ascension onward. Its reach encompasses the universes, parallel histories, timelines, higher planes, and subordinate realms contained within All of Creation. Emanation can render previous physical laws, temporal conditions, causal relationships, dimensions, concepts and dualities obsolete, replacing their operation with the conditions established by the God’s personal truth. This is a transformation of the governing reality itself, exceeding the establishment of a Reality Marble or the exercise of an extraordinary technique within an otherwise independently defined World. The effects of a Hegemony Law therefore cannot be evaluated solely through the resistance normally used against magecraft. The God establishes the conditions under which an event, resistance, transition, or consequence becomes meaningful throughout all of creation. Because everything painted by the Law becomes an extension of the ruler’s will, the God possesses omnipresence and omniscience within that territory. A realm locally belonging to another being does not automatically escape this relationship when it remains a subsection of the same creation. Perception is not dependent upon placing a sensory organ at every point, and the presence of a visible divine body does not restrict the God to that location. Nevertheless, the qualification of territory remains significant: divine awareness throughout the painted creation does not independently establish exhaustive knowledge of 「　」 or unrestricted access to every function associated with it. The individual mechanics of a Law are determined by its possessor. Mercurius’s Acta Est Fabula establishes Eternal Rcurrence across all of creation, returning its infinite universes, parallel worlds and timelines to the instant of his enthronement when he rejects the conclusion of his existence. Its renewal can operate beyond ordinary time and causality, restore those whose existences have been erased, and activate even after his death. Renichi’s Law establishes Eternal Stagnation at the level of events and transitions, extending beyond a conventional suspension of clocks to prevent change even where ordinary time is nonexistent. Its expression includes Timelight Armor, through which external changes cannot meaningfully reach him unless the opposing existence possesses sufficient standing to overcome the Law. Reinhardt’s Law expresses Eternal Warfare through his Legion, its resurrection, its empowerment, and the sovereign relationship connecting its warriors to him. These are individual expressions of godhood rather than techniques automatically shared by every Throne God. Their respective Laws can also be manifested as Kaminagara, cosmic embodiments that externalize the God’s inner universe and provide another form through which divine authority becomes present. A Throne God may additionally possess magecraft, weapons, personal techniques, or specialized manifestations acquired through their individual history; Taikyoku amplifies their standing without erasing the distinction between those personal accomplishments and the foundational Law. Conflict between Gods can produce a Singular Finite Curve leading toward the Throne, although creating the rupture, traversing it, and surviving the approach remain separate undertakings. The Judgement of Mortality encountered there belongs to 「The Throne」 itself, rather than being an ordinary personal attack possessed by every God. Similarly, the Throne’s preservation of an established creation after its ruler’s death is a stabilizing function of the nucleus. It prevents the disappearance of the ruler from automatically erasing the world already painted, allowing that order to persist until a successor establishes another Hegemony Law.

Anatomy: The anatomy of a Throne God is most accurately approached through the relationships composing their divine existence. No universal arrangement of human organs, supernatural tissues, or magic circuits establishes membership in this condition. A humanoid form may retain recognizable features, but its appearance provides insufficient evidence that an ordinary heart, brain, or circulation constitutes the indispensable center of the God’s survival. The individual consciousness remains significant because the Law belongs to someone: a particular being whose Origin supplies its principle and whose Craving determines its desired expression. Their soul and Origin are fundamental to this identity and to the density of Taikyoku, while their inner universe represents the reality emanated through that foundation. These are meaningful distinctions for description, although treating them as completely separate objects would misrepresent their integration. The painted creation extends the God’s will without confining the God to the sum of its contents. Destroying an inhabitant, a planet, or a local texture therefore does not automatically constitute a wound to an indispensable divine organ. Descriptions requiring an attacker to shave away an entire universe to damage such an existence indicate the inadequacy of local bodily injury as a model; cosmological scope must still be accompanied by the ability to interact with the God’s metaphysical standing. Kaminagara provides the clearest outward expression of this anatomy. It is the physical manifestation of the Hegemony Law, bringing forth the God’s inner universe or multiverse in a form corresponding to their own nature. Its magnitude can be immense, and its visible scale does not exhaust the reality it expresses. Mercurius’s Kaminagara takes the form of a colossal ouroboric celestial dragon surrounded by astronomical structures, Reinhardt’s manifests as an enormous golden lion associated with his Legion, and Renichi’s appears through an immense arrangement of clocks and celestial machinery. Within the Kaminagara, the governing Law possesses no blind spots. Injury to this manifestation produces limited feedback upon the God, compared to the bite of a venomless snake, so it should not be understood as a conveniently externalized heart whose destruction necessarily ends its source. Mercurius’s Sensories constitute a different anatomical relationship. These are remotely controlled interfaces described as complete nothingness, allowing his engagement with creation while his true self remains seated. They possess their own diminished level of manifested power, retain access to his knowledge and magecraft, and permit selective interaction with material phenomena. Destroying one does not injure Mercurius’s true existence, because the terminal is not individually necessary to his survival. His sense of self includes these nonlocal manifestations without making each one an independent person. The Legion’s integration with Reinhardt likewise demonstrates that divine anatomy can encompass numerous souls and warriors as essential expressions of a single God’s Craving. Such arrangements remain individual characteristics. The existence of Sensories, a Legion, a particular Kaminagara, or a source-dependent form of incomplete godhood cannot be assigned universally to every possessor of Taikyoku.

Hegemony Law: A Hegemony Law is the absolute governing order produced when a Throne God’s Origin attains Taikyoku and emanates outward, establishing the God’s internal reality throughout the creation encompassed by their dominion. Its fundamental principle originates from the God’s Origin, while its particular arrangement and desired outcome are determined by their Craving. Through this relationship, the orientation of a single individual becomes the condition under which innumerable other existences are permitted to operate. Physics, causality, time, the relationship between life and death, and the applicability of concepts throughout the painted creation become subject to the reality established by the God. The process is described as painting because the former World becomes a canvas upon which the God’s own truth is expressed, with the resulting universes, histories, and subordinate realms becoming extensions of their will. This metaphor does not adequately describe the full nature of the transformation, but it conveys the relationship between the ruler, the established Law, and the creation receiving it. A Hegemony Law possesses authority over the terms of an event’s occurrence, extending beyond the production of extraordinary phenomena within an otherwise independently functioning universe. A phenomenon that contradicts the established Law cannot secure an exception merely by operating according to the previous laws of nature, because the authority of those laws has itself been superseded. If an Origin of Water reaches Taikyoku and establishes its principle throughout creation, the resulting water cannot be compelled to evaporate or freeze through subordinate physical processes; the conditions determining those processes now proceed from the supreme principle defining the world. The precise expression differs between Gods because an Origin does not independently supply every detail of its orchestration. Mercurius’s Origin of Unknown, directed by his refusal to accept any conclusion other than his desired ending, becomes Acta Est Fabula, the Law governing Heaven Lost. Its recurrence encompasses infinite parallel universes and timelines, renewing creation when the conclusion of his existence fails to satisfy him. Its operation supersedes ordinary temporal sequence and causality, allowing renewal even after his death and restoring existences for which conventional recovery would be impossible. Renichi’s Origin of Stagnation, orchestrated through his wish to remain within a cherished moment, establishes a condition preventing change throughout creation, extending to events and transitions even where ordinary time is absent. Reinhardt’s Origin of Love expresses itself through the eternal warfare, resurrection, and sovereign unity of his Legion. The similarity of their divine standing therefore does not make their Laws interchangeable. Each is the cosmological expression of a particular individual’s supreme principle and desire. Wherever a ruling God’s Law has been established, the God is omnipresent and omniscient within that territory because the painted creation is an extension of their will. A realm belonging locally to another being remains subject to this relationship if it is still contained within the same painted creation. 「The Throne」 stabilizes the ruling God’s emanation, contains the otherwise destructive pressure of their presence, and preserves the established order. Its support also means that the death of the ruler does not automatically erase the world already painted; that world can persist until a successor establishes a new Hegemony Law. Candidates and incomplete Gods may possess Taikyoku and manifest their Laws before attaining the full conditions of enthronement, so possession of a Law and unrestricted rulership must be evaluated separately. The Law’s absoluteness concerns its supremacy within the creation it governs. A competing Law possessing sufficient Taikyoku can contest and overwrite it, while an existence with a separately established relationship to 「　」 may constitute an explicit exception. Its authority never makes the God sovereign over Akasha itself. 「The Throne」 remains intrinsic to the Root, and the Law established through it remains the expression of a particular God whose individuality has not disappeared into the Absolute Origin.

Kaminagara: Kaminagara is the manifest embodiment of a Throne God’s Hegemony Law, externalizing the universe or multiverse intrinsic to their divine existence in a form corresponding to their own nature. The God’s internal reality, already capable of determining the conditions of creation through emanation, becomes perceptible as a cosmic manifestation through which that same Law is expressed. Its appearance is consequently inseparable from the principle and desire of its possessor. The great difference in scale between a human observer and a Kaminagara reflects the inadequacy of treating the God as a humanoid organism whose full existence occupies the dimensions of an ordinary body. Although Kaminagara are typically immense, their visible magnitude does not exhaust the reality they embody, and Mercurius’s manifestation is explicitly capable of adjusting its apparent size. Their presentation supplies an intelligible form for something whose underlying significance cannot be adequately measured by its silhouette. Mercurius’s Kaminagara appears as an enormous celestial dragon arranged in ouroboric coils, accompanied by astronomical rings, occult diagrams, and the imagery of a cosmos revolving around its divine observer. Its form expresses recurrence, the ordered return of histories, and the sovereign position of the individual who rejects every unacceptable ending. Reinhardt’s manifests as a colossal golden lion whose radiance encompasses the warriors of his Legion, presenting eternal warfare as a unified divine existence gathered around its king. Renichi’s appears through a vast celestial mechanism of clocks, interlocking gears, dials, and astronomical instruments, with his figure enthroned at its center. Its form expresses his dominion over the moment and his desire to preserve the existence gathered within it. These appearances are meaningful expressions of their respective Laws, although the represented creatures and mechanisms should not be reduced to ordinary biological or mechanical constructions. Within the Kaminagara, the God’s Law possesses no blind spots. The manifestation belongs to the same supreme principle that establishes its interior conditions, and concealing oneself behind one of its apparent structures does not independently create a region beyond that principle. This completeness concerns the expression and dominion of its own Law; it does not establish exhaustive knowledge of 「　」 or unrestricted access to every function associated with the Root. Because Kaminagara is genuinely connected to the God, damaging it can affect its possessor. However, the feedback is limited, compared to the bite of a venomless snake, and the manifestation cannot be treated as an exposed vital organ whose destruction necessarily terminates the entire divine existence. A meaningful attack must still establish the capacity to contend with the Law being embodied. Apparent proximity, great physical size, or an identifiable head does not make ordinary anatomical assumptions sufficient. A confrontation between Kaminagara is therefore significant insofar as it expresses the collision of their Gods’ respective Laws and Taikyoku. The visible exchange may resemble a battle between colossal entities, while its decisive relationship concerns the supremacy of the realities manifesting through them. Kaminagara must also be distinguished from a Sensory. Mercurius’s Sensories are diminished interfaces through which he engages with the World while remaining seated, and their destruction leaves his true self unharmed. Kaminagara externalizes the God’s Law and inner universe, with a different relationship between manifestation and source. Neither the properties of a remote terminal nor the weaknesses of an ordinary familiar can be assigned to it solely because all three can appear as something standing before an observer.

Craving: Craving is the profound personal desire that determines the orchestration of a Throne God’s Origin as a Hegemony Law. It supplies the direction through which a supreme principle becomes a particular world, expressing what the individual ultimately wishes existence to become. The Origin establishes the fundamental orientation; Craving gives that orientation its personal purpose and desired arrangement. A God’s Law is therefore cosmological in scope while remaining intimately connected to the person whose desire produced it. The greater the reach of their emanation, the more extensively other existences must live within the consequences of that desire. Craving cannot be adequately understood as a temporary instruction, a casually spoken wish, or a strategic preference selected for a single battle. Its significance comes from its depth within the individual’s existence and its relationship with the Origin that becomes Taikyoku. Possessing a powerful desire alone does not establish godhood, and momentary emotional intensity does not provide a universal means of overcoming every disparity in Taikyoku. The weight of desire contributes to the density of the supreme principle, alongside the strength of the soul and Origin and the particular mechanisms through which the individual accumulates power. Once expressed as Law, however, a personal longing can become an objective condition imposed upon creation irrespective of whether its inhabitants share it. Mercurius’s Craving illustrates this relationship through his refusal to accept any ending other than the conclusion he desires. His Origin of Unknown supplies the fundamental principle, while his demand for an acceptable unknown ending determines the recurrence through which he continues rejecting familiar or unsatisfactory conclusions. The Law preserves his ability to try again, yet also prolongs the repetition from which he seeks release. His desire therefore contains the psychological contradiction of a God who wishes to encounter genuine novelty while continually renewing the creation he already knows. The power of his Craving does not guarantee that the desired encounter will occur or that another person will freely reciprocate his wishes. Renichi’s Craving expresses a different contradiction. His wish to remain forever within a cherished moment possesses the potential to halt every event and transition, preserving what he loves while preventing further development. Nevertheless, the people he loves possess desires of their own, and his respect for their autonomy becomes significant to the Law’s orchestration. His ability to select those exempted from its stagnation expresses that personal qualification. During his earlier development, the distinction between wishing to preserve a moment and allowing himself to belong within that moment obstructed the coherent manifestation of his Law. He approached permanence while continuing to position himself as a temporary visitor, producing a contradiction between his stated longing and the way he understood his own place in life. Resolving that conflict allowed him to express his existing Origin and Craving more coherently; it did not require the conclusion that his Taikyoku had simply vanished. Reinhardt’s Craving similarly gives his Origin of Love a particular cosmological expression through eternal warfare and the embrace of his Legion. His affection, admiration of resistance, and desire for unending battle become inseparable in the world his Law would establish. Craving thus determines why the attainment of divine power does not erase personality, attachment, fear, or dissatisfaction. Those qualities remain capable of shaping the operation of supremacy itself. Their consequences may include hesitation, an accepted condition of defeat, or an ultimate longing for release from eternity. A God’s subconscious desire for an ending can give rise to a True Death Cell, a manifestation within their own existential system capable of approaching the standing required to destroy its originator. Craving consequently contributes both to the establishment of divine authority and to the personal conditions under which that authority may eventually be surrendered, overcome, or brought to its desired conclusion.

Origin: An Origin is the fundamental orientation of an existence, establishing the direction from which its particular nature proceeds. It belongs to the relationship between the individual and the source from which differentiated existence emerges, and its significance extends more deeply than a learned discipline, an acquired technique, or a consciously adopted ambition. An individual may remain unaware of their Origin while continuing to be influenced by it. Awakening that Origin strengthens its direct relationship with the bearer, allowing the fundamental orientation to exert greater influence over the person’s existence. Within the order maintained by 「The Throne」, the distinction between Origin and bearer permits that orientation and the eventual individual to remain meaningfully distinguishable. Origins emerging from 「　」 are stabilized and maintained in dormancy before their bearers are born, preventing the fundamental direction from immediately overwhelming the particular person who will possess it. Awakening weakens that distinction without necessarily abolishing it. The Origin remains fundamental, while the bearer remains an individual whose experiences, relationships, and choices determine how their life develops. Attaining Taikyoku constitutes a further transformation in this relationship. The Origin becomes supreme and emanates outward, directing creation according to the individual’s fundamental principle. What previously oriented one existence becomes capable of establishing the conditions under which countless existences proceed. An ordinary awakening and the attainment of a Supreme Ultimate Origin therefore cannot be treated as equivalent accomplishments. The decisive change concerns the Origin’s standing and scope, together with the God’s independence from the ordinary frameworks it supersedes. The Origin supplies the principle of a Hegemony Law, but its name alone does not provide a complete account of the world that will result. Craving remains necessary to explain its orchestration. Unknown does not, by its ordinary linguistic meaning, independently specify the entire mechanism of Mercurius’s recurrence; Stagnation does not by itself explain Renichi’s attachments or the exemptions through which he preserves the autonomy of chosen people; Love does not independently describe the particular eternal warfare embodied by Reinhardt. Their Laws express the union of fundamental orientation and individual desire. This also means that outwardly related effects do not prove identical Origins, while apparently contradictory behavior may require understanding the bearer more deeply before the relationship becomes clear. The God remains the person whose principle is being expressed, and the principle becomes cosmological without exhausting every aspect of that person’s identity. At the level of Taikyoku, the distinction between the individual’s Origin and 「　」 becomes infinitesimally small. 「The Throne」 maintains the remaining distinction so that supreme emanation can coexist with an independent consciousness. The Supreme Ultimate Origin remains a particular orientation, while the Absolute Origin is the undifferentiated source from which all such orientations emerge and to which they ultimately return. A Throne God’s extraordinary proximity to the Root consequently does not establish ownership of it. The stabilization of Origins throughout creation is a function intrinsic to the Throne’s relationship with Akasha, and occupying that mechanism does not transform the ruler into the source of Akasha itself. The remaining distinction is indispensable to their condition as a God who desires and governs. Should it disappear completely, the individual would cease to remain distinguishable from 「　」, together with the separate consciousness through which their Origin possessed a personal direction and their Craving sought a particular world.

Weaknesses: The principal vulnerability of a Throne God is confrontation by an existence capable of reaching the level at which their Origin and Law are established. Ordinarily, another God possessing comparable or greater Taikyoku supplies this capacity directly. Their battle becomes a collision of supreme principles, in which the weaker Law can be painted over, crushed, and consumed together with the existence expressing it. The resulting termination is conventionally described as death, although it does not require ordinary mortality to have become applicable beforehand. The victor overcomes the defeated God’s entire divine standing. Density, compatibility, and the relationship between the opposing Cravings matter in such a confrontation, while a sufficiently great numerical disparity cannot be overcome merely by increasing the spectacle of a subordinate attack. An ability that destroys souls, erases concepts, or imposes death must still establish that its operation reaches Taikyoku. Its description alone does not supply the necessary interaction. 「Shiki Ryougi」 constitutes an explicit exception through her direct relationship with 「　」 and her Origin of Nothingness, allowing her to harm Throne Gods without possessing a Taikyoku value of her own. This exception concerns her particular existence and does not automatically extend to every Root-connected individual or every wielder of death perception. Another fundamental vulnerability is the True Death Cell, a self-destructive mechanism arising within a God’s own soul through their subconscious desire for an ending. Its manifestation can develop sufficient standing to confront the originator, becoming a personal embodiment of termination where the ordinary Concept of Death has no jurisdiction. The term “cell” expresses its relationship with the God’s existence; it does not establish an ordinary infection that an opponent can introduce into divine tissue. Reinhardt exemplifies this mechanism in relation to Mercurius. His source-dependent nature permits him to confront his originator and prevents ordinary destruction of his body, soul, or manifestation from guaranteeing permanent death while Mercurius remains. The same relationship binds his final fate to that source: when Mercurius receives his true final death, Reinhardt perishes alongside him. His full divine confrontation with Mercurius also does not constitute a universal ability to equalize himself with every possible opponent. Renichi’s incomplete godhood possesses a separate dependence upon Mercurius, whose true death may cost him half of that godhood until he claims the Throne. Such dependencies must be evaluated individually rather than treated as a common vulnerability shared by every God. Personal psychology remains equally important because Craving determines the orchestration of divine supremacy. An unresolved contradiction between what a person desires and how they actually position themselves within life can obstruct the manifestation of their Law, as occurred during Renichi’s earlier development. Resolving that contradiction restores coherence without requiring the conclusion that he had lost his Taikyoku, and a resolved developmental conflict should not be retained as a permanent disability. Mercurius’s desire for an acceptable unknown conclusion demonstrates another limitation: power over recurrence cannot manufacture the personal fulfillment he seeks. His rejection of endings makes his behavior intelligible through that desire, while the recurrence itself means that defeating him does not necessarily establish a permanent outcome. His paradoxical existence additionally prevents his use of the Memory of the Throne and Akashic Records, a restriction belonging to his own condition rather than to Throne Gods universally. Their supremacy also requires the stabilizing relationship with 「The Throne」 to be understood correctly. The seat supports the ruling God’s emanation and preserves the distinction preventing their Origin from becoming indistinguishable from 「　」; this does not establish a simple tactic by which removing an apparent chair kills the ruler. Nor does the greater strength of a Throne God make them sovereign over the Root itself. Their individuality persists through the final distinction maintained before complete return, and eliminating that distinction would eliminate the separate consciousness whose Origin, Craving, and Law constitute the God.
